      "hints": [
        "Set $3\\sin^2 2x + 2 = 4 \\implies \\sin 2x = \\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$.",
        "Find four angles in radians to 3 s.f."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Set $p = 3, q = 2$ and solve $f(x) = 4$:\n$$3\\sin^2 2x + 2 = 4 \\implies 3\\sin^2 2x = 2 \\implies \\sin^2 2x = \\frac{2}{3}$$\n$$\\sin 2x = \\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\approx \\pm 0.81650$$",
        "Find the reference angle in radians:\n$$\\alpha = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\right) \\approx 0.95532\\text{ radians}$$",
        "Determine all four solutions for $2x \\in [0, 2\\pi]$ and divide by $2$:\n$$2x_1 = 0.95532 \\implies x_1 \\approx 0.478$$\n$$2x_2 = \\pi - 0.95532 = 2.18628 \\implies x_2 \\approx 1.09$$\n$$2x_3 = \\pi + 0.95532 = 4.09691 \\implies x_3 \\approx 2.05$$\n$$2x_4 = 2\\pi - 0.95532 = 5.32787 \\implies x_4 \\approx 2.66$$\n$$(\\text{Solutions: } x = 0.478, \\quad 1.09, \\quad 2.05, \\quad 2.66)$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
